"constitution | Wex | US Law | LII / Legal Information Institute (https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/constitution)\nciteturn21view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/constitution\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 30\nL0: Please help us improve our site!\nL1: \nL2: 【0†No thank you】\nL3: # constitution\nL4: \nL5: A constitution is the most fundamental law of a sovereign body. The term is capitalized only when referring to a specific constitution (e.g., 【1†U.S. Constitution】, 【2†Texas Constitution†tlc.texas.gov】, etc.).\nL6: In many cases, \"constitution\" refers to a single written document that explicitly creates government institutions, defines the scope of government power, and guarantees certain 【3†civil liberties】. Nonetheless, a constitution does not inherently need to be written. In the case of an unwritten constitution, the term may refer to the general structure of a certain government regardless of how it arose or evolved. For example, the United Kingdom does not have a written constitution.\nL7: [Last reviewed in May of 2026 by the 【4†Wex Definitions Team†about.law.cornell.edu】]\nL8: \nL9: Wex\nL10: \nL11:   * 【5†CIVICS】\nL12:   * 【6†the Constitution】\nL13:   * 【7†wex definitions】\nL14:   * 【8†constitutional law】\nL15:   * 【9†government】\nL16:   * 【10†legal education and practice】\nL17: ## Wex Toolbox\nL18: \nL19: * * *\nL20: \nL21: * * *\nL22: \nL23:   * 【11†Accessibility】\nL24:   * 【12†About LII】\nL25:   * 【13†Contact us】\nL26:   * 【14†Advertise here】\nL27:   * 【15†Help】\nL28:   * 【16†Terms of use】\nL29:   * 【17†Privacy】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHow laws are made | USAGov (https://www.usa.gov/how-laws-are-made)\nciteturn21view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.usa.gov/how-laws-are-made\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 98\nL0: 【0†GTM†www.googletagmanager.com】 【1†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: 【2†Image: Close】\nL3: \nL4:   * 【3†All topics and services】\nL5:   * 【4†The U.S. and its government】\nL6:   * 【5†Government benefits】\nL7:   * 【6†Immigration and U.S. citizenship】\nL8:   * 【7†Money and credit】\nL9:   * 【8†Travel】\nL10:   * 【9†Voting and elections】\nL11:   * 【10†Laws and legal issues】\nL12:     * 【11†Request copies of vital records and ID cards】\nL13:     * 【12†Find a lawyer and affordable legal aid】\nL14:     * 【13†Family legal issues】\nL15:     * 【14†Prisons and prisoners】\nL16:     * 【15†Crime reporting, victim support, and crime statistics】\nL17:     * 【16†Authenticate an official document】\nL18:     * 【17†State attorneys general】\nL19:     * 【18†Federal laws and regulations】\nL20:       * 【19†Research federal laws】\nL21:       * current page\nL22: \nL23: How laws are made\nL24:       * 【20†Your government files through the Privacy Act】\nL25:       * 【21†Copyrighted government works】\nL26:       * 【22†Impeachment process】\nL27:     * 【23†Federal, state, territory, county, and municipal courts】\nL28: # How laws are made\nL29: \nL30: Congress is the lawmaking branch of the federal government. Learn how a bill becomes a law and how the process is different in the House of Representatives and in the Senate.\nL31: ## How a bill becomes a law\nL32: \nL33:   1. A bill is a proposal for a new law or a change to an existing law. The idea for a bill can come from a:\nL34:      * Sitting member of the U.S. Senate or House of Representatives\nL35:      * Proposal during a congressional candidate's election campaign\nL36:      * Petition by people or citizen groups who recommend a new or amended law to a member of Congress that represents them\nL37:   2. Once a bill is introduced, it is assigned to a committee whose members will research, discuss, and make changes to the bill.\nL38: \nL39:   3. The bill is then put before that chamber to be voted on.\nL40:   4. If the bill passes one body of Congress, it goes to the other body to go through a similar process of research, discussion, changes, and voting.\nL41: \nL42:   5. Once both bodies vote to accept a bill, they must work out any differences between the two versions. Then both chambers vote on the same version of the bill. If it passes, they present it to the president.\nL43:   6. The president then considers the bill. The president can approve the bill and sign it into law. Or the president can refuse to approve a bill. This is called a veto.\nL44:   7. If the president chooses to veto a bill, in most cases Congress can vote to override that veto and the bill becomes a law. But if the president does not sign off on a bill and it remains unsigned when Congress is no longer in session, the bill will be vetoed by default. This action is called a pocket veto, and it cannot be overridden by Congress.\nL45: \nL46: 【24†Learn more about the U.S. Congress.†www.visitthecapitol.gov】\nL47: ## How the House and Senate’s lawmaking procedures are different\nL48: \nL49: The Senate and the House have some procedural differences. While both are equal in how they function:\nL50: \nL51:   * Only the House can initiate tax and revenue-related legislation\nL52:   * Only the Senate can draft legislation related to presidential nominations and treaties\nL53:   * While the House processes legislation through a majority vote, the Senate does so through deliberation and debate prior to voting.\nL54: 【25†Learn more about the legislative process with this video from Congress.gov.†www.congress.gov】\nL55: \nL56: LAST UPDATED: November 17, 2025\nL57: \nL58: SHARE THIS PAGE:\nL59: ## Have a question?\nL60: \nL61: Ask a real person any government-related question for free. They will get you the answer or let you know where to find it.\nL62: \nL63: 【26†Call USAGov】\nL64: \nL65: 【27†Chat with USAGov】\nL66: \nL67: 【28†Top】\nL68: \nL69: ### Government information\nL70: \nL71:   * 【3†All topics and services】\nL72:   * 【29†Directory of U.S. government agencies and departments】\nL73:   * 【30†Branches of government】\nL74: \nL75: ### About us\nL76: \nL77:   * 【31†About USAGov】\nL78:   * 【32†Report a website issue】\nL79:   * 【33†Website usage data】\nL80: ### For federal agencies\nL81: \nL82:   * 【34†Partner with us】\nL83:   * 【35†Read our blog】\nL84: #### USAGov Contact Center\nL85: \nL86: Ask USA.gov a question at\nL87: \nL88: 【26†1-844-USAGOV1 (1-844-872-4681)】\nL89: \nL90: USAGov is the official guide to government information and services\nL91: \nL92: An official website of the 【36†U.S. General Services Administration†www.gsa.gov】\nL93: \nL94: This website complies with Executive Order 【37†14224†www.federalregister.gov】. English is the official language of the U.S. Government, and the English-language version of this website is the authoritative source for all federal information contained herein.\nL95:   * 【38†Accessibility policy】\nL96:   * 【39†Privacy and security policies】\nL97:   * 【40†FOIA requests†www.gsa.gov】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn21view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.kremlin.ru/acts/constitution\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://www.kremlin.ru/acts/constitution: (400) Timeout fetching--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ntreaty | Wex | US Law | LII / Legal Information Institute (https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/treaty)\nciteturn21view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/treaty\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 27\nL0: Please help us improve our site!\nL1: \nL2: 【0†No thank you】\nL3: # treaty\nL4: \nL5: A treaty is a formally signed and 【1†ratified】 【2†agreement】 between two or more nations or sovereigns; a 【3†contract】 between two or more countries that is adhered to by the nations party to it; an international agreement between two or more states that is governed by 【4†international law】.\nL6: In the United States, treaties are federal law and thus 【5†preempt】 state law. The treaty power is granted by 【6†Article II, Section 2 of the Constitution】, under which the President may make a treaty by and with the advice and consent of the Senate, with the concurrence of two-thirds of those present. To circumvent this requirement, a President may enter informal treaties by executive agreements with the leaders of other nations.\nL7: [Last reviewed in August of 2021 by the 【7†Wex Definitions Team†about.law.cornell.edu】]\nL8: \nL9: Wex\nL10: \nL11:   * 【8†the Constitution】\nL12:   * 【9†wex definitions】\nL13:   * 【10†international law】\nL14: ## Wex Toolbox\nL15: \nL16: * * *\nL17: \nL18: * * *\nL19: \nL20:   * 【11†Accessibility】\nL21:   * 【12†About LII】\nL22:   * 【13†Contact us】\nL23:   * 【14†Advertise here】\nL24:   * 【15†Help】\nL25:   * 【16†Terms of use】\nL26:   * 【17†Privacy】\n"
